<Hub pillarId="safety-debates">
  <p>People say “superintelligence” as if it were a product on a shelf. It is not. The careful report uses tighter words, and the experts in it do not agree. We will not sand that into a slogan.</p>
  <h2>What are people arguing about?</h2>
  <p>
    Public conversation often uses the word “superintelligence” for a system that would far outperform people. The <a href="https://internationalaisafetyreport.org/sites/default/files/2026-02/international-ai-safety-report-2026.pdf">International AI Safety Report 2026</a> does its work with tighter terms. It defines artificial general intelligence as a hypothetical model or system that equals or surpasses human performance on all or almost all cognitive tasks. It defines a loss-of-control scenario as one in which one or more general-purpose systems operate outside of anyone’s control, with no clear path to regaining control.
  </p>
  <p>
    The report says some experts give credence to outcomes as severe as the marginalisation or extinction of humanity, and that other experts consider such scenarios implausible. Disagreement, it says, comes from disagreements about future capabilities, behavioural propensities, and how systems will be deployed. We are not going to pretend that split is closed.
  </p>
  <h2>What does the report say about today?</h2>
  <p>
    Current systems, the report says, show early signs of relevant capabilities, but not at levels that would enable loss of control. A system would need to evade oversight, carry out long-term plans, and stop people from putting countermeasures in place. The report says loss of control becomes more likely if a system is misaligned: if it uses its capabilities in ways that conflict with the intentions of developers, users, or society. Alignment, in the report’s glossary, is the propensity to use capabilities in line with human intentions, values, or norms. The report calls alignment an open scientific problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    Present harm is a different chapter. The report says current systems already fail in ordinary ways, including fabricated information, and that malicious actors already use generated content for scams. Those are documented. A future loss of control is a risk the report treats as uncertain in likelihood and potentially extreme in severity.
  </p>
  <p>
    NIST’s <a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework">AI Risk Management Framework</a>, released on 26 January 2023, is a voluntary way for organisations to manage risks and to build trustworthiness into design and use. It is not a verdict on the long-term debate. The <a href="https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles">OECD principles</a> ask for AI that is robust, secure, and safe, and for accountability. They are standards for practice, not a prediction.
  </p>
  <p>Words: <a href="/glossary/#artificial-general-intelligence">AGI</a>, <a href="/glossary/#alignment">alignment</a>, <a href="/glossary/#loss-of-control">loss of control</a>.</p>
